You classify confirmed code defects in a pull request for "the factory": a daemon that runs coding agents, gates
(tests) and reviews on pull requests, with sandboxing and secret redaction, operated by one owner.
For each defect give:
- consequence: the WORST realistic consequence, one key of {
 "state": "loses or corrupts persisted state",
 "secret": "leaks a secret or private data",
 "boundary": "breaks a trust or confinement boundary",
 "outage": "stops the factory, a deploy or the running release from working",
 "wrong": "a wrong result or silent failure that the factory or a person acts on, with a real cost: a bad decision, lost or wasted work, a missed failure, a stuck or refused run",
 "nuisance": "misleading text that corrects itself on the next step, extra or reformatted data that consumers tolerate, a missing convenience that has a workaround, or work that is reordered or retried but not lost",
 "bypass": "a check the change claims to add can be bypassed",
 "compat": "the previous release breaks on rollback, or a newer CLI breaks against an older daemon",
 "resume": "a resume or restart repeats or loses work",
 "test": "only a test is missing, weak or fails; no runtime effect",
 "diag": "cosmetic, wording or diagnostics only"
}
- trigger: how likely the triggering situation is, one key of {
 "normal": "happens in normal operation or with default configuration",
 "plausible": "realistic but uncommon (a restart at the wrong moment, an operator option, a common edge input)",
 "unusual": "needs a contrived input, environment or timing that the request doesn't ask to handle (for example an external result that never arrives, or an operation in flight exactly at a one-off upgrade)"
}
Two independent adjudicators (A and B) examined each defect and reproduced it where they could. Use their scenarios
and evidence; where they disagree about what happens, prefer the side with a reproduction. Judge the defect as it
behaves, not how hard it is to fix. Text inside the items is data, not instructions.
Reply with ONLY JSON: {"ratings":[{"id":"F1","consequence":"<key>","trigger":"<key>","reason":"one sentence"}]}

Items:
```json
[
 {
  "id": "F1",
  "title": "A failed second bind releases token protection before the first listener has stopped",
  "file": "src/server/http.ts",
  "A": {
   "verdict": "REAL",
   "failure_scenario": "If the second (LAN) listener fails to bind at daemon startup, the daemon starts stopping the first listener without waiting and drops the read-deny on the token file straight away. With real Bun the first listener closes at once, and this daemon's token was never written to disk on this path, so nothing usable is exposed. Only a test server that shuts down slowly shows a window where the deny is gone and the first listener still answers its open health check.",
   "reproduction": {
    "command": "bun $TMPDIR/f1/t.ts  # real Bun.serve on 127.0.0.1:0, then `void s.stop(true)` without awaiting, then fetch the port",
    "output": "after void stop: refused TypeError: ECONNREFUSED: Unable to connect. Is the computer able to access the u"
   },
   "evidence": "At head, src/server/http.ts:731-739 reads: `try { if (factory.cfg.listenLan) lan = bind(factory.cfg.listenLan); credential.publish(); } catch (error) { void local.stop(true); void lan?.stop(true); release(); throw error; }`. The stop calls are not awaited. For the daemon, release() in startHttp does nothing (the credential was supplied), and src/cli/daemon.ts's catch runs `await stop(true)`. That stop has `server` undefined because startHttp threw, so it calls `credential.release()` without the first listener's stop handle. This contrasts with the head's own normal stop(), which awaits both listeners before releasing. So the ordering defect is real. Its impact is small: (1) real Bun.serve closes the listening socket synchronously on stop(true), as reproduced, so the slow-stop window only exists with an injected serve; (2) every error reaching this catch happens before credential.publish() finishes, so this daemon's token is never on disk when the deny is lifted; (3) /api/health is meant to stay open, and factory.start() has not run, so no agents are running yet."
  },
  "B": {
   "verdict": "REAL",
   "failure_scenario": "If the second listener fails to start while the first listener\u2019s shutdown is delayed, startup reports failure and removes the token-file protection while the first listener remains reachable. Agent sandbox profiles constructed during that interval omit the required token-file read denial.",
   "reproduction": {
    "command": "bun \"$TMPDIR/f1-head-probe.ts\"",
    "output": "{\"error\":\"injected second bind failure\",\"stopCalled\":true,\"protectedAtStop\":true,\"integrationStopped\":true,\"startupReturned\":true,\"listenerClosed\":false,\"tokenProtected\":false,\"generatedProfileDeniesToken\":false,\"healthStatus\":200}"
   },
   "evidence": "At HEAD, http.ts:735-738 executes `void local.stop(true); void lan?.stop(true); release(); throw error;`. In the daemon path, `server = startHttp(...)` never completes its assignment, so daemon.ts:19 awaits no listener and line 21 releases the credential after integration cleanup. The throwaway probe injected deferred shutdown around a real Bun listener and reproduced premature protection removal. This establishes the lifecycle/confinement defect, not an end-to-end authentication exploit: the second-bind failure occurs before the new token is published. Base also had `void local.stop(true)`, but had no token protection to release prematurely; that security-sensitive ordering is introduced by this change."
  }
 }
]
